  "contract": "The HTTP schedule surface of a fresh namespace. Laravel validation names, messages and counts for creation; duplicate, invalid-cron and empty-spec refusals; a yearly cron schedule in Europe/Budapest and a paused buffer_all schedule mixing two cron expressions with an offset interval in America/New_York, each with PHP's next fire time; pausing and resuming with notes; manual triggers of all six overlap policies three times while no worker runs the started workflows, with buffering, a full buffer and closing the other run; a two-run quota that deletes the schedule; an overlap override, pausing twice and a skipped paused trigger; history pages; and deletion. Every response matches published PHP's apart from generated identities and wall-clock times other than next fire times. Published PHP on SQLite can fail a trigger with database is locked against its own maintenance roles; the whole trigger rolls back, so the probe retries exactly that failure, and replays the whole sequence in a new namespace when a response records it instead, which native must never need. Scheduler-driven fires, update, listing and backfill are separate fixtures. Embedded applications have no HTTP schedule surface."
